WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.840
In 2026, US unemployment rate is currently 4.3%.
If unemployment isn’t high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.080
why is the job market so awful?
It feels like it's never been harder to get a job.&nbsp;

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.720
The story is stranger than we might think.
Total employment.&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.600
Full time.
Part time.&nbsp;

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.240
Something is happening that&nbsp;
we haven't seen before.&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.560
Why do things feel so bad if the&nbsp;
unemployment rate is pretty good?&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.960
And why are thousands of companies posting job&nbsp;
listings they have no intention of filling?

00:00:53.960 --> 00:01:01.160
Now the instinct for many people is to say:&nbsp;
It's AI! AI is causing all these problems!&nbsp;

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.040
And there's truth to that.
But, there's more going on here.&nbsp;

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.600
First, let's look at what AI is doing.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:15.560
For one, lots of layoffs and redundancies.
1,500 from Meta, then another 8,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:23.000
1,600 from Atlassian, 4,760 from PayPal,&nbsp;
16,000 from Amazon, 30,000 from Oracle!&nbsp;

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.840
So far this year, there's&nbsp;
been over 300,000 layoffs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.440
over 85,000 from tech alone. A&nbsp;
33% jump from this time last year.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.800
Feels especially bad when CEOs refer to&nbsp;
layoffs as "lower-value human capital".&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:44.840
But more than that, AI is doing something else.
A 2026 study from BCG said that "over the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:51.840
two to three years, 50% to 55% of jobs in the US&nbsp;
will be reshaped by Ai. They retain the same or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.520
similar role but face radically new expectations&nbsp;
for how they work and what they produce"&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.680
Perhaps the most worrying part is entry&nbsp;
level jobs, which are starting to decline.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:08.160
“Postings for entry-level jobs in the US&nbsp;
have plummeted by 35% in the last 18 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.040
largely thanks to AI”

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:15.080
So it seems particularly bad for those trying to&nbsp;
get into the workforce through roles like these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:20.520
as the more basic tasks which require less&nbsp;
experience, are the ones most vulnerable to AI.&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.240
While it sucks to be working in tech right now,&nbsp;
this AI displacement doesn’t seem to have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.000
exponential growth people were saying it would.
"Initial evidence suggests that transitional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.840
disruption from AI to date is not outpacing recent&nbsp;
technological changes. A similar pace to the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:43.280
after the start of the commercial computer era&nbsp;
(1984) and the commercial Internet era (1996) and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.960
has not accelerated since the release of ChatGPT."
According to a survey of over 2,000 businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.200
across 9 different countries, including&nbsp;
the US, "42% say AI has created jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.520
across their organizations, 11% say it&nbsp;
has eliminated roles, and 35% report a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:06.080
mix of both — netting to 77% reporting job&nbsp;
creation versus 46% reporting job loss"&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.640
Though, these are responses from companies,&nbsp;
so take that with a grain of salt.&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.200
But, there is something that shows&nbsp;
this is more than just AI, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.840
it's causing other problems we'll get to soon.
But AI doesn’t explain everything outside of tech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:28.160
A lot of jobs that can't be replaced by AI,&nbsp;
seem to be the ones in the worst situation.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.040
Most assume blue-collar jobs would be doing great.&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.280
Some people are saying "Leave white-collar,&nbsp;
move into trades, you'll get a job".&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.440
"AI can't replace a plumber!"
And that's true, but that's not what's happening.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.840
According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.440
the industries with the worst change in hiring&nbsp;
were trade, construction, and federal jobs.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.400
Look at that graph. Information, professional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.960
and financial are showing fewer hires in the last&nbsp;
few years, but it's not just white-collar jobs.&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.320
Interestingly, healthcare is the industry with&nbsp;
the most explosive growth. Healthcare represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:12.000
47.5% of all job growth during 2025.
So what's going on, really?

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.720
With the current job market, a lot of&nbsp;
people have to re-evaluate their career.&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.760
It feels like the market is&nbsp;
redefining what a "job" even means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.320
and many are deciding to try running a business.&nbsp;

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.280
Business applications are at&nbsp;
record highs, and it makes sense!

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.440
More and more businesses are&nbsp;
being started out of necessity.&nbsp;

