00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.520
The labor market is in a very weird spot.&nbsp;

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:21.400
Unemployment isn't skyrocketing… but&nbsp;
new jobs added are historically low.

00:06:50.957 --> 00:06:53.797
The total number of employees is mostly flat.

00:06:53.797 --> 00:07:00.117
We're essentially in a "freeze". A&nbsp;
low-hire, but also… a low-fire market.

00:07:00.117 --> 00:07:03.877
The hiring rate has gradually been&nbsp;
decreasing for the past few years.

00:07:03.877 --> 00:07:09.677
In February 2026 this hit 3.1, about the same&nbsp;
as the collapse at the start of the pandemic.&nbsp;

00:07:09.677 --> 00:07:15.877
Fewer companies are hiring people, though, it's&nbsp;
floated around this low point for a few years now.&nbsp;

00:07:15.877 --> 00:07:22.397
But interestingly, also firing fewer.
Job openings are around 7 million. Down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.397 --> 00:07:30.037
a lot from a few years ago, and now, quite flat.
But, the job opening rate is also flat at 4.1%.&nbsp;

00:07:30.037 --> 00:07:34.237
"The number and rate of layoffs and&nbsp;
discharges were little changed at 1.9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.237 --> 00:07:41.037
million and 1.2 percent. Layoffs and discharges&nbsp;
were little changed in all industries in March”

00:07:41.037 --> 00:07:44.757
What's happening is that companies&nbsp;
aren't keen to hire more staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.757 --> 00:07:50.157
but aren't so keen to lay off people, as the&nbsp;
cost of replacing an employee is quite high.&nbsp;

00:07:50.157 --> 00:07:54.277
Not only that, staff aren't keen&nbsp;
to leave their jobs right now.

00:07:54.277 --> 00:07:58.517
Fewer people are keen to quit, which&nbsp;
creates even fewer job openings.&nbsp;

00:07:58.517 --> 00:08:02.197
[Jerome Powell - Federal Reserve Chair]: "It's&nbsp;
an unusual and uncomfortable kind of a balance"&nbsp;

00:08:02.757 --> 00:08:08.557
So, why are we in a low-hire, low-fire&nbsp;
situation? Well, a few reasons.&nbsp;

00:08:09.157 --> 00:08:15.157
First, I think this is a pendulum swing after&nbsp;
Covid. There was a hiring boom after the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.157 --> 00:08:20.797
and perhaps a lot of companies overhired.
Another is perhaps the recent tariffs.&nbsp;

00:08:21.317 --> 00:08:24.797
These increased the costs of doing&nbsp;
business, more expensive parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.797 --> 00:08:28.717
lower margins, less profit.
Less desire to hire.&nbsp;

00:08:29.397 --> 00:08:32.211
And it's likely to stay this way for a while.
[Mitchell Barnes, labor markets economist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:36.317
The Conference Board.]: "We should expect [low&nbsp;
hire low fire] to be more the norm going forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.317 --> 00:08:40.877
We have really been marking our calendars for the&nbsp;
first half of 2026 for really the full kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.877 --> 00:08:46.437
suite of the tariff impacts to really roll through&nbsp;
to businesses and, therefore, the labor market."&nbsp;

00:08:46.437 --> 00:08:52.557
But this "low-hire, low-fire" market feels&nbsp;
especially bad, when we take another perspective.

00:08:53.117 --> 00:08:57.997
Total jobs are steady,
But, this, along with a lot of "jobs added" stats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.997 --> 00:09:01.707
misses one important nuance.
Full time, vs part time.

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:02.957
Full time.
Part time.&nbsp;

00:09:02.957 --> 00:09:08.197
Full-time employment is either flat&nbsp;
or declining, part-time is going up.&nbsp;

00:09:08.197 --> 00:09:12.197
"The labor market's success has been&nbsp;
fueled mostly by part-time-job growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.197 --> 00:09:16.757
rather than by the addition of full-time&nbsp;
jobs. The household survey suggests an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.757 --> 00:09:21.037
explanation for why Americans don't&nbsp;
perceive a robust labor market:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.037 --> 00:09:27.357
from their point of view, it isn't robust."
People might not be fired, but instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.357 --> 00:09:32.117
their job might be "changed".
Moved from full time to part time.&nbsp;

00:09:32.117 --> 00:09:36.117
Or a company might remove one full-time&nbsp;
job, but add two more part-time.&nbsp;

00:09:36.797 --> 00:09:41.757
On the surface, it looks like&nbsp;
job growth, but it's not.&nbsp;

00:09:41.757 --> 00:09:45.877
It's those who are unemployed and trying&nbsp;
to find work, who have it worse than ever.&nbsp;

00:09:46.477 --> 00:09:52.917
The market is terrible for job hunters right now.&nbsp;
The actual process of getting a job is I'd say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.917 --> 00:09:57.677
the worst it has ever been in&nbsp;
history. For a lot of reasons.

