dives into economics, companies, and finance.&nbsp;

00:10:12.117 --> 00:10:15.877
There's a lot these stories&nbsp;
can teach us! So subscribe!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.877 --> 00:10:19.877
We're almost at 1 million now,&nbsp;
and you can help us get there!

00:10:19.877 --> 00:10:28.237
Applicants per job posting in 2021, was about&nbsp;
45. In 2025, it was 95. More than double.&nbsp;

00:10:28.237 --> 00:10:34.317
With a low hiring rate, more and more people&nbsp;
are applying for those jobs that are out there.&nbsp;

00:10:34.317 --> 00:10:39.517
But, to make matters worse, many&nbsp;
of these jobs… don't even exist.

00:10:39.517 --> 00:10:43.637
Essentially, you find a job listing,&nbsp;
from a real, legitimate company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.637 --> 00:10:49.357
it has all the components of a normal job, like&nbsp;
responsibilities, experience needed, everything.&nbsp;

00:10:49.357 --> 00:10:52.837
Looks legit! You write a cover&nbsp;
letter, send your resume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.837 --> 00:10:59.077
maybe send a follow-up email a few weeks later.
Except… this job doesn't even exist.&nbsp;

00:10:59.077 --> 00:11:03.117
These are ghost jobs.
Fake listings by real companies.&nbsp;

00:11:03.677 --> 00:11:08.277
These have existed for a while,&nbsp;
but it's getting much, much worse.&nbsp;

00:11:08.917 --> 00:11:16.877
Hires per job has fallen from 0.75 in 2018, to&nbsp;
below 0.5 in 2023. It's likely even lower today.&nbsp;

00:11:17.477 --> 00:11:21.157
That means, less than HALF of job&nbsp;
postings will result in a hire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.677 --> 00:11:27.117
Not that you're less likely to get that&nbsp;
job, that anyone will be hired for that job.&nbsp;

00:11:27.717 --> 00:11:31.877
Surprisingly, those blue-collar jobs like&nbsp;
construction are being hit the worst.&nbsp;

00:11:32.717 --> 00:11:38.077
Construction has it the worst, with 38%&nbsp;
of postings classified as ghost jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.077 --> 00:11:43.157
followed by the art industry, and&nbsp;
then legal jobs at 34% and 29%.

00:11:43.677 --> 00:11:49.397
Administration and support has seen a&nbsp;
negative 55% change in hires to job postings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.397 --> 00:11:54.877
followed closely, again, by construction.
A 2024 survey found that "39% of hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.877 --> 00:12:00.277
managers admitted their companies posted fake&nbsp;
job listings in the past year. Among those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.277 --> 00:12:07.157
26% said they posted one to three fake listings,&nbsp;
while 13% reported posting 75 or more."&nbsp;

00:12:07.677 --> 00:12:11.637
So, why do this?
This seems like such a waste of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.637 --> 00:12:16.997
not just for applicants, but even the companies.
Well, there's a few reasons.&nbsp;

00:12:16.997 --> 00:12:21.917
First, many publicly traded companies&nbsp;
will post fake job listings, not for jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.917 --> 00:12:26.757
but as part of their PR strategy.
A 2024 study found that almost "1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.757 --> 00:12:32.157
in 4 recruiters were asked to keep postings&nbsp;
live "for visibility," not active hiring."&nbsp;

00:12:32.157 --> 00:12:37.837
In another survey, 23% said it helped make the&nbsp;
company appear more stable during a hiring freeze.&nbsp;

00:12:37.837 --> 00:12:40.677
If you look like you're hiring,&nbsp;
it implies you're growing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.677 --> 00:12:46.837
doing well, and investment is more appealing.
For public companies, and even private companies.&nbsp;

00:12:47.757 --> 00:12:52.997
But the second reason is even worse.
Another survey found 62% of hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.997 --> 00:12:57.277
managers use fake job listings, to&nbsp;
"have employees feel replaceable".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.797 --> 00:13:03.037
That's right. To make employees work harder, and&nbsp;
make them feel like they have less job security.&nbsp;

00:13:03.037 --> 00:13:07.917
So, they're not just harming people looking&nbsp;
for work, but people inside the company.&nbsp;

00:13:08.437 --> 00:13:13.597
The third reason is compliance requirements.
If the company already has someone in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.597 --> 00:13:17.797
who's actually the perfect fit for the&nbsp;
job, who's worked there for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.797 --> 00:13:21.797
they still have to go through the song&nbsp;
and dance of "pretending to hire".

00:13:21.797 --> 00:13:25.517
Hiring within a company is a good&nbsp;
thing. Letting employees move up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.517 --> 00:13:29.237
or maybe horizontally to a&nbsp;
more enjoyable job, is great.&nbsp;

00:13:29.237 --> 00:13:33.237
But, they still create a job&nbsp;
listing, and waste everyone's time.

00:13:33.797 --> 00:13:37.837
Recruiters and HR, to be honest, feel&nbsp;
like the villains in a lot of this.&nbsp;

00:13:37.837 --> 00:13:42.517
People are using AI to apply for jobs, and&nbsp;
recruiters are using AI to hire people.&nbsp;

00:13:42.517 --> 00:13:48.597
87% of companies and 99% of the Fortune&nbsp;
500 are using it in their hiring processes.&nbsp;

00:13:49.157 --> 00:13:55.677
65% of recruiters rely on it daily and&nbsp;
24% use it as their primary hiring method.&nbsp;

00:13:55.677 --> 00:13:59.837
81% for candidate sourcing,&nbsp;
73% for resume screening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.837 --> 00:14:05.117
68% candidate communication via chatbots.
And you can see why.&nbsp;

00:14:05.957 --> 00:14:12.397
It's 30-40% cheaper, and time to hire is up 50%.
But boy does it suck to go through.&nbsp;

00:14:13.237 --> 00:14:17.357
No one is looking at any job applications,&nbsp;
and people are getting rejection emails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.357 --> 00:14:22.877
before anyone even looks at their resume.
It's especially bad, because hiring doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.877 --> 00:14:28.317
often fall to a team lead, but HR.
A manager might identify the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.317 --> 00:14:33.277
possible candidate, but they&nbsp;
don't actually matter: HR does.&nbsp;

00:14:33.277 --> 00:14:38.077
This is why you sometimes get weird skill&nbsp;
requirements on a job listing or in an interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.077 --> 00:14:41.637
because recruiters, often, have no&nbsp;
idea what the job actually involves.&nbsp;

00:14:42.157 --> 00:14:47.677
Not to mention, it's especially bad with&nbsp;
some companies just lying on job listings.

00:14:47.677 --> 00:14:53.397
Listing a job as "remote", then saying it’s 'an&nbsp;
onsite position and requires in-office presence",

00:14:53.397 --> 00:14:58.357
Even if unemployment isn't growing much,&nbsp;
it's not a great time to be job hunting.&nbsp;

00:14:58.357 --> 00:15:01.837
Especially as people are stuck&nbsp;
unemployed for longer and longer.

00:15:01.837 --> 00:15:07.077
Many are applying for hundreds, or even&nbsp;
thousands of jobs, and getting nowhere.&nbsp;

00:15:07.677 --> 00:15:13.277
This is made worse by companies abusing the job&nbsp;
postings to do something it was never meant for.&nbsp;

00:15:13.957 --> 00:15:18.357
Something definitely needs to change,&nbsp;
potentially new legislation to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.357 --> 00:15:22.877
the job market is doing what it's supposed&nbsp;
to: Giving people a chance to be hired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.877 --> 00:15:27.997
giving companies a chance to find a new&nbsp;
employee, when there's actually a free position.&nbsp;

00:15:27.997 --> 00:15:33.077
Not for PR, to scare employees, or just&nbsp;
because they legally have to post it.

00:15:33.837 --> 00:15:37.197
One piece of good news though,&nbsp;
is that AI replacements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.197 --> 00:15:38.917
aren't working as well as many thought.&nbsp;

00:15:39.437 --> 00:15:44.157
AI customer service, AI layoffs, are&nbsp;
actually backfiring for a lot of companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.157 --> 00:15:48.437
and CEOs are starting to regret it.
